you  culture just exists whether you want it  or not if you don't manage it properly  you might create negative and  counterproductive cultures that don't  really emphasize the things that are  going to serve your organization  culture was readily dismissed up until  the late 1970s as something that  managers don't need to care about but as  organizations and work becomes more  complex and people no longer work on an  assembly line when you can basically  procedurally tell them what to do and it  doesn't really matter what they think  because the assembly line solves all the  dilemmas for them as as robots are  replacing these and people are put more  and more in positions where they need to  make decisions there's no way we can  exhaustively kind of describe all the  situations that employees are going to  to deal with and that's where culture  comes into play it's when there are  these implicit understandings about what  is important and what isn't important  and what we're trying to maximize and  what we're not trying to maximize and  who we are and who we aren't and there's  also this kind of moral code that holds  us together then you would expect that  on average employees would do the right  thing even if you haven't specified  precisely the recipe of how to do it so  that's why culture is becoming  increasingly more important than  organizations and is increasingly  recognized by leaders as something that  they need to invest in  we basically find that people follow one  of three trajectories either they show a  gradual increase in their level of  cultural fit so basically they're slowly  learning the code and behaving  appropriately these are people who stay  in the organization and succeed we see  people who never really show an increase  in their level of cultural fit relative  to what they were immediately after  joining the organization so that  indicates to us that they're either  incapable or unwilling to adapt  culturally and those are the people at  extreme risk of being fired so basically  these people if they survive a year and  they rarely do they have a 50% chance of  being fired by that time and finally we  find I think the most interesting group  of people are those who demonstrate this  inverted u-shape these people who are  capable of adapting but at some stage  start showing a decline in their level  of cultural fit and that's a strong  indicator of these people's intention to  leave voluntarily so really we see these  different signatures in terms of  people's and culture agent trajectories  and how those relate to their outcomes  in in the organization  so imagine like a group of software  engineers that all sit together in the  same room and pay play ping pong and  after hours and know one another those  type of groups can really tolerate the  non conformist weirdo and that might  actually be a very good thing for the  organization because that weirdo so to  speak right this person who's not  behaving in the appropriate way is  probably also a reflection of the fact  that that person is bringing new ideas  and new sensitivities and has different  ways of thinking of problems and that  might be really important for the  organization's success especially if  this organization needs some innovation  and and a new thoughts in order to to be  successful with its product offerings so  you know we find that there's this  trade-off between your ability or  tendency to behave in a compliant way  but also the security relaxed data or  thereof that you gain from the extent to  which you're part of a titanate group or  not and really being in in the right  kind of sweet spot leads to the greatest  success in organizations  on average having a strong culture is a  good thing if everybody if everybody is  aligned on the same important values and  if we would expect therefore that faced  with the same kind of situational  dilemmas everybody would solve them in  roughly the same way and in a way that's  conducive to the organization's strategy  then yes a strong culture is going to be  beneficial for the organization but at  the same time you don't want echo  chambers especially if you're an  organization that competes on novelty if  you're a Google or an aspiring Google or  if you're in Amazon or if you're even  innovating in retail and if you're  innovating in transportation you don't  want your organization to be an echo  chamber you want to empower your  employees to also take risks sometimes  and think outside of the box  some organizations are architected in a  way where actually people aren't part of  strong groups the kind of crisscross  different functional roles and teams in  a matrix kind of structure so in those  organizations people would probably put  a strong emphasis on being culturally  compliant and there will be strong  rewards to strong cultures that  everybody is aligned around however you  can imagine other types of organizations  that are built of strongly connected  groups where inside the group people  have very strong familiarity within it  with one another and very intimate with  one another in those groups you can  afford or those individuals can't afford  to behave in a culturally non-compliant  way and it might be the case that those  groups if they have a good mix of people  who are compliant and non-compliant are  the ones who might come up with the most  brilliant and innovative ideas so it's  not that there's a kind of  one-size-fit-all recipe that facilitates  how culture kind of translates into into  good operational results from an  organizational point of view different  organizations different organizational  architectures and different strategic  imperatives prescribe different and  intricate relationships between  structure and culture in the  organization

